# draw.io diagram standards

## Canvas
- Light canvas only. Never reference or assume a dark background.
- Use: page="0", grid="1", gridSize="10" (infinite canvas for docs).

## Visual style
- Light-card style: white rounded rectangles for inner content blocks, soft pastel containers/swimlanes.
- Text is always dark: #111827 or #1a1a1a. Never light text on light boxes.
- Node text 14–16px. Section/swimlane titles 18–20px.
- Inner blocks large enough that text isn't cramped; comfortable padding.
- Inner blocks should visually fill their container — no huge dead space, no disconnected-island lanes.
- Diagram must be readable at 75% zoom.
- Short node text: title + 2–4 short lines.

## Connector colors (by SOURCE lane)
- UI / Entry = blue
- Logic / Core = green
- Data / Assets = gold/yellow
- State / Auth = purple
- AI / External Services = red/pink
- Backend / API = orange or teal
- Hosting / Deployment = gray
Apply the source-lane color to the stroke (and badge accent, if used).

## Routing (build it right the first time — these are generation constraints, not a cleanup pass)
- Orthogonal connectors only. Prefer short direct routes; 2–4 bends max.
- Connectors may cross each other and may pass through lane gaps, top gutters, and lower buses.
- Connectors may NOT pass through the interior of any white content block — route around them.
- Long cross-lane routes use canvas gutters above or below swimlanes — never through unrelated swimlane bodies.
- A "top gutter" route goes literally above the swimlane title bars, not between the title and the first block.
- A "lower bus" goes below the outer containers, not through their interiors.
- Don't route everything through one bus — split flows across top, middle, and bottom.
- Stagger parallel routes by 12–20px. Never stack parallel connectors on top of each other.
- If a route can't be clean, move the block slightly. If a relationship is obvious from grouping, drop the arrow.
- Avoid intra-lane arrows between adjacent blocks unless the relationship is genuinely important.

## Arrow endpoints
- Attach to the nearest logical side of source and destination.
- Don't dump arrowheads on the top of a block when the line approaches from the side/bottom.
- Stagger endpoints when multiple arrows hit the same block; never land two on the same coordinate.

## Line styles
- Solid colored arrow = confirmed main data/control flow.
- Dashed colored arrow = optional / inferred / external / needs-verification.
- No dotted lines unless the legend defines them. strokeWidth=3 for main connectors.

## Labels & legend
- Avoid text labels on arrows. Use numbered badges in small white rounded rectangles with dark text, placed near (not on) the connector.
- Always include a legend explaining: numbered markers, solid vs dashed meaning, source-lane colors, and any project-specific terms (e.g. BYOK).

## draw.io XML (fallback only — skip if the MCP builds via tool calls)
- Root must include <mxCell id="0"/> and <mxCell id="1" parent="0"/>.
- Unique IDs; every non-root cell has a valid parent.
- Vertices have vertex="1"; edges have edge="1" with source/target referencing existing vertex IDs.
- Define all vertices before edges. Every <mxGeometry> has as="geometry".
- Escape XML: &amp; not &; no stray < or > in labels; html=1 for labels with line breaks.

## When editing an existing .drawio file
- Preserve existing pages and content unless told otherwise.
- Prefer moving/resizing/restyling/rerouting over regenerating from scratch.
- Confirm the file still opens in draw.io.
